1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:02,712
How Do Vaccines Protect Us?

2
00:00:02,662 --> 00:00:19,500
A vaccine induces adaptive immunity by presenting the immune system with antigens derived from a pathogen—either weakened live organisms, inactivated pathogens, purified protein subunits, or genetic material encoding antigenic proteins—without causing the disease itself.

3
00:00:19,500 --> 00:00:24,675
Edward Jenner's 1796 experiment marks the foundation of vaccinology.

4
00:00:24,675 --> 00:00:38,737
Jenner observed that milkmaids who had contracted cowpox (Variolae vaccinae), a mild zoonotic orthopoxvirus infection, appeared immune to smallpox (Variola major, which killed approximately 30% of those infected).

5
00:00:38,737 --> 00:00:46,612
He inoculated eight-year-old James Phipps with material from a milkmaid's cowpox lesion; Phipps developed mild symptoms and recovered.

6
00:00:46,612 --> 00:00:56,250
Two months later, Jenner exposed Phipps to smallpox; the boy exhibited no symptoms, demonstrating cross-protective immunity between related viruses.

7
00:00:56,250 --> 00:01:01,575
The immunological mechanism involves both humoral and cell-mediated responses.

8
00:01:01,575 --> 00:01:11,837
After vaccination, antigen-presenting cells process the antigen and display fragments via major histocompatibility complex molecules to naive T cells in lymph nodes.

9
00:01:11,837 --> 00:01:23,012
This activates B cells to produce neutralizing antibodies (primarily immunoglobulin G) and to differentiate into memory B cells that persist for decades in bone marrow niches.

10
00:01:23,012 --> 00:01:28,662
Cytotoxic T cells provide cellular immunity against intracellular pathogens.

11
00:01:28,662 --> 00:01:32,375
Vaccine platforms have diversified significantly.

12
00:01:32,375 --> 00:01:41,625
Live-attenuated vaccines (measles, mumps, rubella, yellow fever) offer robust immunity but require strict cold-chain storage.

13
00:01:41,625 --> 00:01:48,587
Inactivated vaccines (polio, hepatitis A) are safer for immunocompromised individuals.

14
00:01:48,587 --> 00:02:02,150
Subunit vaccines (hepatitis B, human papillomavirus) use purified antigens combined with adjuvants—aluminum salts or MF59—that enhance immunogenicity by stimulating Toll-like receptors.

15
00:02:02,150 --> 00:02:20,062
The US Food and Drug Administration's emergency use authorizations of the first mRNA vaccines (BNT162b2 from Pfizer-BioNTech and mRNA-1273 from Moderna) in December 2020 for COVID-19 represented a historic technological breakthrough.

16
00:02:20,062 --> 00:02:35,250
These vaccines encapsulate nucleoside-modified messenger RNA within lipid nanoparticles, directing host cells to produce the SARS-CoV-2 spike protein, triggering B-cell and T-cell responses without exposing the body to the live virus.

17
00:02:35,250 --> 00:02:39,687
Global vaccination campaigns have achieved historic successes.

18
00:02:39,687 --> 00:02:47,400
The World Health Organization certified smallpox eradication in 1980 following a global ring-vaccination campaign.

19
00:02:47,400 --> 00:02:55,412
Polio cases have declined by over 99.9% since 1988, with only two endemic countries remaining.

20
00:02:55,412 --> 00:03:05,150
mRNA technology is now under investigation for influenza, cytomegalovirus, cytomegalovirus, and personalized cancer vaccines.

